gm-method (fix-verify under confirmed multi-agent browser-slot contention): when the task brief pre-warns of a single shared browser-verb slot across concurrent sibling dispatches, and repeated attempts (8-12+, varying session names/single-shot scripts/contention-checks) show session ids alternating mid-turn, a DIFFERENT concurrent agent's console/script content bleeding into this dispatch's response, and native UV_HANDLE_CLOSING crashes -- this is the documented contention class, not a broken tool. Correct resolution: stop contending (further attempts risk corrupting a sibling's in-flight measurement, the exact hazard flagged), and substitute a STATIC EQUIVALENCE PROOF for the live before/after when the change is a provable no-behavior-change optimization (e.g. a dirty-value-cache wrapping existing gl.uniform*/similar calls: run a tiny node -e harness simulating the skip-logic against both a static-value sequence, which must collapse to 1 call, and a changing-value sequence, which must upload every time with the SAME value the old always-upload code produced) -- this proves the new code never emits a different value than the old code, only elides same-value redundant re-uploads. Combine with node --check (syntax) and a mechanical collision-audit against any pre-existing cache this change reuses. Partial live reads obtained during contention (even from a shared/interleaved session) that show pageErrors:[] and a plausible fps are corroborating, not required. Record the exhausted attempts + static proof as the mutable witness_evidence, resolve, and proceed -- do not block EMIT/commit on an unattainable clean live A/B when the task brief itself names this as an expected multi-agent hazard.